// The machine's core count, exposed to kama. `parallel_for` has always sized its worker fan-out from
// kama_parfor_workers(); this is the same number, asked for directly, so a program that builds its own
// pool and one that hands work to `parallel_for` agree about the machine.
//
// The assertion has to hold on every leg, and the legs disagree about the answer: native reports the real
// count (10 on the machine this was written on), wasm reports what the browser/node offers, and bare metal
// reports 1 by construction. So the fixture asserts the INVARIANT — at least one, and the same answer
// twice, since a per-call query that drifted would be worse than useless for sizing a pool.
import { std::concurrent::cpuCount };

fn int32 main() {
    int32 n = cpuCount();
    if (n < 1) { return 1; }
    if (cpuCount() != n) { return 2; }   // stable across calls — a pool is sized from it once
    return 0;
}
